From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mcla-x86 SDK patch layer
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] 0720: hdr_fxaa: FXAA (swap_post_effect) also runs on the HDR10 image

Source: Ours, written for this SDK (stream HDR, mission 2). The SDK's FXAA pass (fxaa_cs / fxaa_extreme_cs, its root signature
  and constants) is used unchanged; DXBC of the two changed refresher variants rebuilt with fxc 10.0.26100 (Renan's recipe).
Licence: GPL-3 (owner's own work, part of mcla-x86); the patched files are ReXGlue SDK code (BSD-3-Clause)
Purpose: With HDR10 output the swap cleared swap_post_effect, so FXAA was lost in HDR. Now (D3D12, no frame generation) the HDR
  refresher (fused and old variants) writes its PQ code values into the FXAA source texture (R16G16B16A16_UNORM) with luma in
  alpha (flag bit 16: dot(pq, 0.299/0.587/0.114), the SDR *_fxaa_luma weights on PQ codes, the perceptual analogue of SDR's
  gamma codes), then the same FXAA pass writes the guest output, exactly as SDR does. Cvar hdr_fxaa (bool, default true, hot
  reload): false = the old behaviour (FXAA skipped in HDR). Log on change: "HDR: FXAA on the HDR10 image (...)" / "... off
  (<reason>)". Skipped with frame generation, a debug view or the test pattern. Vulkan unchanged (FXAA stays off in HDR there).
Bucket: REDO

Run h2-6-val (gpu_timing groups, medians, frame-exact entries): swap group HDR 730 -> 1268 us with FXAA (+0.54 ms), SDR 200 ->
743 us (+0.54 ms): the same cost. Parked dumps, edges (3x3 contrast > 60 PQ codes, 19 % of pixels): FXAA changes 33.8 % of them
in HDR (mean 11.2 codes) vs 29.3 % in SDR (11.8); p99.9 unchanged. Known: FXAA's sub-pixel blend in PQ dims 1-px glints more
than SDR's in gamma (406 -> 95 nits vs 291 -> 110): they end at the SDR+FXAA level.
---
